  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1150 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nSaturday...Moist westerly flow aloft and a weak shortwave trough \naloft near peak heating will combine with deep atmospheric moisture \nsupply to produce scattered to numerous showers and scattered \nthunderstorms in the afternoon and evening, with the greatest areal \ncoverage east of Interstate 25. Aiding in the lift will be low level \nsoutheast winds adding the mechanical lift in addition to diurnal \nheating. PWAT, Precipitable water values look decent for beneficial \nrainfall. 700 mb temperatures yield highs in the 70s to lower 80s.\n\nSunday...Ridging becomes a bit more pronounced over Wyoming, \nproducing warmer temperatures aloft which will tend to limit areal \ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms, compared to Saturday. \nNevertheless though, the monsoonal moisture will stretch from \ncentral Colorado through far eastern Wyoming and western Nebraska, \nwhere scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms will occur in \nthe afternoon and evening. Slightly warmer, but comfortable, \ntemperatures for this time in August.\n\nMonday...A drier pattern ensues and is on tap as the flow aloft \nturns west northwest and the precipitable water values decrease, due \nto drying at low and mid levels and downslope southwest winds near \nthe surface. Thus, a much drier day when compared to the weekend. A \nwarming trend begins with more sunshine and warmer mid level \ntemperatures.\n\nTuesday...Brisk westerly flow aloft prevails, and with a decent \namount of low and mid level moisture availability, expect isolated \nto widely scattered late day showers and thunderstorms, mainly east \nof Interstate 25 and the Laramie Range. Similar maximum temperatures \nto Monday based on 700 mb temperatures.\n\nWednesday...The synoptic pattern looks more like September and early \nautumn with a deep shortwave trough aloft sweeping across western \nMontana and Idaho, with west southwest flow aloft over our counties. \nRight now, we have scattered late day showers and thunderstorms in \nour gridded forecasts, though with a decent increase in precipitable \nwater values, the moist flow aloft, and low level upslope, along \nwith pattern recognition, believe our POPS and convective coverage \nwill likely need to be increased in the next few days. \n\nThursday...The high amplitude and progressive shortwave trough aloft \nmoves eastward from western Montana, to the Dakotas and western \nNebraska by evening, sending a decent Pacific origin cold front \nacross our counties. There may be enough moisture at low and mid \nlevels for isolated to widely scattered convective coverage, though \nthe biggest change will likely be a return to cooler temperatures \nand windy downslope west winds, which will likely need to be \nadjusted in the next few days, if model consistency remains for day \n7. If you are looking for an early taste of fall weather, this may \nbe your day.",
